Yeah. The ball’s already off the bat, but the swing is still telling you how it got there.

And if you tell a kid to finish the swing, that cue can help before contact. The finish they’re aiming for changes how they move through the ball. You’re coaching an earlier moment by giving them something to do a beat later.

Then there’s the observer. Show someone just the moment of contact, then show them the whole swing. Same hit, but the finish may make it look deliberate, lucky, or off-balance. And you can pose a beautiful finish after a lousy swing. That’s where it gets especially interesting for the Pantheon. The visible ending can reveal how an action happened, and it can also be staged to make you remember it differently.